  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nCurrently, west/northwest flow aloft with upper level ridging\nover the western CONUS and a long wave trough over\ncentral/eastern Canada. Strong surface high pressure continues \ndeveloping east across the Dakotas, with a steady northwest \nbreeze and a mostly clear sky over the region along to ahead of\nthis feature.\n\nSfc high will continue east tonight, with winds becoming\nlight and variable with time (along with some southerly return \nflow west). Some increase in mid/upper level clouds ahead of an \nembedded mid level impulse moving into the Northern Rockies. \nLows tonight again dropping into the 30s, with frost developing \nespecially in the more prone areas. \n\nThe above mentioned mid level wave continues east for Friday,\nmoving across northern areas of the region and contributing to\nlow chances for showers Fri afternoon north and east. Expect\nmoisture reaching the surface to be minimal. Return flow also\nincreases in the wake of the sfc high, with a breezy\nsouth/southeasterly wind during the day tomorrow. \n\nWe remain in a steady west/northwesterly flow aloft this \nweekend, maintaining mainly dry weather and near normal \ntemperatures. Western CONUS ridge nudges east into the Great \nPlains as it deamplifies early next week, but will provide for \ntemperatures trending to above normal Monday/Tuesday, when highs\nwarm well into the 70s to around 80. The only concern regarding\nfire weather will be over the southwest, where RH values are \nprojected lowest next week (20-25 percent), and if this is \ncoupled with any elevated afternoon winds.",
